arXiv:2510.21345v1 [cs.LG] 24 Oct 2025</div>
</div><div class="ltx_page_content">
<article class="ltx_document ltx_authors_1line">
<h1 class="ltx_title ltx_title_document"><math id="m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>-LoRA: Effective Fine-Tuning via Base Model Rescaling</h1>
<div class="ltx_authors">
<span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Aymane El Firdoussi
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>MLO, EPFL
</span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">El Mahdi Chayti
</span><span id="id1" class="ltx_note ltx_note_frontmatter ltx_thanks_contribution ltx_role_thanks"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_type">thanks: </span>Equal contribution</span></span></span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>MLO, EPFL
</span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Mohamed El Amine Seddik<sup id="id2" class="ltx_sup">*</sup>
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>TII, UAE
</span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Martin Jaggi
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>MLO, EPFL
</span></span></span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">Fine-tuning has proven to be highly effective in adapting pre-trained models to perform better on new desired tasks with minimal data samples. Among the most widely used approaches are reparameterization methods, which update a target module by augmenting its frozen weight matrix with an additional trainable weight matrix. The most prominent example is Low Rank Adaption (LoRA) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hu et al., 2022</a>)</cite>, which gained significant attention in recent years. In this paper, we introduce a new class of reparameterization methods for transfer learning, designed to enhance the generalization ability of fine-tuned models. We establish the effectiveness of our approach in a high-dimensional binary classification setting using tools from Random Matrix Theory, and further validate our theoretical findings through more realistic experiments, such as fine-tuning LLMs.</p>
  
</div>
<div id="p1" class="ltx_para">
<table id="p1.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_align_top">
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="p1.1.1" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_rule" style="width:0.0pt;height:24.0pt;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"></span></td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Large foundational models have driven major advances in artificial intelligence across domains such as computer vision and natural language processing. Examples include transformer-based models <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">Vaswani et al., 2017</a>)</cite> operating in natural language domain <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">Team et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Grattafiori et al., 2024</a>)</cite> or vision domain <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cordonnier et al., 2020</a>; <a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dosovitskiy et al., 2020</a>)</cite>. Such models are specifically known for their relatively large size and massive training corpus, which makes them more powerful and adapted for many use cases. However, even with their extensive pre-training, these large models may not excel at some specific tasks without further adjustment. 
<br class="ltx_break">Fine-tuning addresses this need by updating a pre-trained model with task-specific data. Unlike training from scratch, it leverages general pre-trained representations while reducing data and compute requirements. The most common class of fine-tuning methods is Supervised Fine-Tuning (SFT), which relies on labeled data in that adaptation process, and one of its most popular lightweight techniques is Low-Rank Adaptation (LoRA) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hu et al., 2022</a>)</cite>, which updates the desired module by adding a low-rank perturbation to the original (frozen) weight matrix. 
<br class="ltx_break">In this paper, we study fine-tuning through the lens of Random Matrix Theory (RMT), where we introduce a theoretical framework to understand and improve transfer learning. Leveraging the theoretical findings, our key practical idea in the context of LoRA is to scale the frozen weights row-wise with a vector <math id="S1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝜶</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math> before adaptation, thereby adding a new degree of freedom to the fine-tuning process. We show that this modification leads to an optimal scaling factor <math id="S1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\alpha}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝜶</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\alpha}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>, which is typically different from the standard choice (<math id="S1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\alpha}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝜶</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\alpha}=1</annotation></semantics></math>). We analyze this framework in a high-dimensional binary classification setting under a Gaussian Mixture Model, proving the existence of such an optimal <math id="S1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\alpha}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝜶</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\alpha}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> while providing its closed-form expression in terms of scalar data-dependent quantities. We then validate our theoretical insights on real tasks, including transfer learning benchmarks and large language model fine-tuning.</p>
</div>
<section id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Summary of contributions.</h4>

<div id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our main contributions are summarized as follows:</p>
<ol id="S1.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In the context of adaptation fine-tuning (e.g., LoRA), we propose the scaling of the base model weight matrices by a non-trivial row-wise vector <math id="S1.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝜶</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We theoretically prove the existence of an optimal parameter <math id="S1.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\alpha}^{*}\neq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝜶</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>≠</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\alpha}^{*}\neq 1</annotation></semantics></math> in high-dimensional binary classification and derive its closed form.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">3.</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We design an algorithm to estimate optimal <math id="S1.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝜶</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math> in complex scenarios such as LLM fine-tuning.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Related work</h2>

<section id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Transfer learning foundations.</h4>

<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Transfer Learning (TL) studies how knowledge acquired in a source task or domain can be reused to improve learning in a related target task. Early surveys <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Pan &amp; Yang, 2009</a>; <a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">Weiss et al., 2016</a>)</cite> outlined key settings such as domain adaptation and multitask learning. Most theoretical works established generalization bounds linking transfer success to source error and distributional divergence <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ben-David et al. (2010)</a></cite>, and showed how shared representations reduce sample complexity <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Maurer et al., 2016</a>; <a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">Tripuraneni et al., 2020</a>)</cite>. More recent studies refined these results under classification and regression settings <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hanneke &amp; Kpotufe, 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2021</a>; <a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Klivans et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kpotufe &amp; Martinet, 2021</a>; <a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cai &amp; Wei, 2021</a>; <a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">Reeve et al., 2021</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Fine-tuning pre-trained models.</h4>

<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">With the advent of large-scale pre-training, fine-tuning has become the dominant strategy for transfer learning. The most popular fine-tuning techniques are Supervised Fine-tuning (SFT) and fine-tuning with Reinforcement Learning (RL). RL-based approaches such as RLHF <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ouyang et al., 2022</a>)</cite>, DPO <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Rafailov et al., 2023</a>)</cite>, GRPO <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ramesh et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Guo et al., 2025</a>)</cite> and their variants are especially popular for reasoning and mathematics tasks, where they often outperform SFT <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shenfeld et al., 2025</a>)</cite>. In this paper, however, we focus on SFT techniques. SFT extends the training of a pre-trained model using labeled data. Because these models are typically large in size, it is common to modify only a small fraction of their parameters while leaving most unchanged. This strategy, known as Parameter-Efficient Fine-Tuning (PEFT) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xu et al., 2023</a>)</cite>, aims to achieve strong performance with minimal parameter updates. PEFT methods are usually grouped into three categories: additive, selective, and reparameterized <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ji et al., 2025</a>)</cite>. Our work centers on the last category.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Reparametrized Fine-tuning.</h4>

<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Reparameterization-based fine-tuning adapts a model by expressing its parameters in an alternative form, commonly through a low-rank decomposition, to reduce training costs, while the full weight matrices are reconstructed for inference. The most common technique in this class is Low Rank Adaptation (LoRA) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hu et al., 2022</a>)</cite>, which introduces small, trainable matrices operating alongside the pre-trained weights to inject task-specific updates without burdening the inference process. Many extensions were proposed to enhance the efficiency of LoRA by either acting on the initialization of the low rank modules <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hayou et al., 2024a</a>)</cite>, their learning rates <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hayou et al., 2024b</a>)</cite>, normalizing the updates <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, setting adaptive ranks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kim et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lu et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, finding optimal placements for LoRA modules <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hayou et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, and more <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2023b</a>; <a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dettmers et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kopiczko et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2023a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">Tian et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jiang et al., 2024</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Problem setting and Background</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To prove the effectiveness of our new family of fine-tuning algorithms, we will theoretically analyze a binary classification setting under a Gaussian Mixture Model (GMM) using tools from Random Matrix Theory (RMT). Through this analysis, we will prove the existence of an optimal scaling parameter <math id="S3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha^{\star}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>α</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha^{\star}</annotation></semantics></math> and derive its exact theoretical formulation for these settings.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1 </span>Theoretical Setting</h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The goal is to fine-tune a linear classifier, initially pretrained on a dataset called <span id="S3.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">source</span>, in order to perform a <span id="S3.SS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">target</span> task given a relatively small target data corpus.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Pre-training phase.</h4>

<div id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We consider that we are given pairs of pre-training (source) data samples <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{(\tilde{\bm{x}}_{i},\tilde{y}_{i})\}_{i=1}^{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{(\tilde{\bm{x}}_{i},\tilde{y}_{i})\}_{i=1}^{N}</annotation></semantics></math> that are distributed, for <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\bm{x}}_{i}\in{\mathcal{C}}_{a}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mi>a</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\bm{x}}_{i}\in{\mathcal{C}}_{a}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a\in\{1,2\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a\in\{1,2\}</annotation></semantics></math>, as follows:</p>
<table id="S3.EGx1" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E1"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\tilde{\bm{x}}_{i}\in{\mathcal{C}}_{a}\quad\Leftrightarrow\quad\begin{cases}\tilde{\bm{x}}_{i}={\bm{\mu}}_{a}+\tilde{\bm{z}}_{i},\quad\tilde{\bm{z}}_{i}\sim{\mathcal{N}}({\bm{0}},{\mathbf{I}}_{p}),\\
\tilde{y}_{i}=(-1)^{a}.\end{cases}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>a</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">⇔</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>𝝁</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>a</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒛</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒛</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∼</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>𝟎</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐈</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>p</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>a</mi></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\tilde{\bm{x}}_{i}\in{\mathcal{C}}_{a}\quad\Leftrightarrow\quad\begin{cases}\tilde{\bm{x}}_{i}={\bm{\mu}}_{a}+\tilde{\bm{z}}_{i},\quad\tilde{\bm{z}}_{i}\sim{\mathcal{N}}({\bm{0}},{\mathbf{I}}_{p}),\\
\tilde{y}_{i}=(-1)^{a}.\end{cases}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">For convenience and without loss of generality, we further assume that <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{\mu}}_{a}=(-1)^{a}{\bm{\mu}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝝁</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>a</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝝁</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{\mu}}_{a}=(-1)^{a}{\bm{\mu}}</annotation></semantics></math> for some vector <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{\mu}}\in{\mathbb{R}}^{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝝁</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>p</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{\mu}}\in{\mathbb{R}}^{p}</annotation></semantics></math>. This setting can be recovered by subtracting <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{{\bm{\mu}}_{1}+{\bm{\mu}}_{2}}{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>𝝁</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>𝝁</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{{\bm{\mu}}_{1}+{\bm{\mu}}_{2}}{2}</annotation></semantics></math> from each data point, as such <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{\mu}}=\frac{{\bm{\mu}}_{2}-{\bm{\mu}}_{1}}{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝝁</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>𝝁</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>𝝁</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{\mu}}=\frac{{\bm{\mu}}_{2}-{\bm{\mu}}_{1}}{2}</annotation></semantics></math> and therefore the SNR <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|{\bm{\mu}}\|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>𝝁</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|{\bm{\mu}}\|</annotation></semantics></math> controls the difficulty of the classification problem, in the sense that large values of <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|{\bm{\mu}}\|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>𝝁</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|{\bm{\mu}}\|</annotation></semantics></math> yield a simple classification problem whereas when <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|{\bm{\mu}}\|\to 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>𝝁</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|{\bm{\mu}}\|\to 0</annotation></semantics></math>, the classification becomes impossible. Denoting <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\mathbf{X}}=\left[\tilde{\bm{x}}_{1},\ldots,\tilde{\bm{x}}_{N}\right]\in{\mathbb{R}}^{p\times N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐗</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>N</mi></msub></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\mathbf{X}}=\left[\tilde{\bm{x}}_{1},\ldots,\tilde{\bm{x}}_{N}\right]\in{\mathbb{R}}^{p\times N}</annotation></semantics></math> the data matrix and <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\bm{y}}=\left[\tilde{y}_{1},\ldots,\tilde{y}_{N}\right]^{\top}\in{\mathbb{R}}^{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>N</mi></msub></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>N</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\bm{y}}=\left[\tilde{y}_{1},\ldots,\tilde{y}_{N}\right]^{\top}\in{\mathbb{R}}^{N}</annotation></semantics></math> the corresponding labels vector, we have in matrix form</p>
<table id="S3.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\mathbf{X}}={\bm{\mu}}\tilde{\bm{y}}^{\top}+\tilde{\mathbf{Z}}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐗</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>𝝁</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐙</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\mathbf{X}}={\bm{\mu}}\tilde{\bm{y}}^{\top}+\tilde{\mathbf{Z}},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.3" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\mathbf{Z}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐙</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\mathbf{Z}}</annotation></semantics></math> is a random matrix with <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{N}}(0,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{N}}(0,1)</annotation></semantics></math> i.i.d. entries.
<br class="ltx_break">We then consider training a classifier, called <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\bm{w}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒘</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\bm{w}}</annotation></semantics></math>, on this source dataset by solving:</p>
<table id="S3.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\min_{{\bm{w}}}\frac{1}{N}\sum_{i=1}^{N}\ell({\bm{w}}^{\top}{\bm{x}}_{i},y_{i})+\tilde{\gamma}\|{\bm{w}}\|_{2}^{2}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><munder><mi>min</mi><mi>𝒘</mi></munder><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>N</mi></mfrac></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></munderover><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝒘</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>γ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>𝒘</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\min_{{\bm{w}}}\frac{1}{N}\sum_{i=1}^{N}\ell({\bm{w}}^{\top}{\bm{x}}_{i},y_{i})+\tilde{\gamma}\|{\bm{w}}\|_{2}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.4" class="ltx_p">for some loss function <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell</annotation></semantics></math> and a positive regularization parameter <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\gamma}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>γ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\gamma}</annotation></semantics></math>. Taking a generic or a non-intuitive loss, such as the binary cross entropy, leads to <span id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">intractable</span> solution <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\bm{w}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒘</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\bm{w}}</annotation></semantics></math>. However, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mai &amp; Liao (2024)</a></cite> show that in the case of a Gaussian mixture data model or more generally a data distribution with finite fourth-order moment, it is possible to optimize such a classifier using the squared (<math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>) loss function, which also gives a closed-form solution to this problem. Thus, taking <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell(x,y)=(x-y)^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell(x,y)=(x-y)^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> leads to the following optimization problem:</p>
<table id="S3.E4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\bm{w}}=\argmin_{\bm{v}}\frac{1}{N}\left\|\tilde{\mathbf{X}}^{\top}{\bm{v}}-\tilde{\bm{y}}\right\|_{2}^{2}+\tilde{\gamma}\|{\bm{v}}\|_{2}^{2}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒘</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>min</mi></mrow><mi>𝐯</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>N</mi></mfrac></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐗</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐯</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>γ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>𝐯</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\bm{w}}=\argmin_{\bm{v}}\frac{1}{N}\left\|\tilde{\mathbf{X}}^{\top}{\bm{v}}-\tilde{\bm{y}}\right\|_{2}^{2}+\tilde{\gamma}\|{\bm{v}}\|_{2}^{2},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.5" class="ltx_p">Which gives us the following solution:</p>
<table id="S3.E5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\bm{w}}=\frac{1}{N}{\mathbf{R}}\tilde{\mathbf{X}}\tilde{\bm{y}},\quad{\mathbf{R}}=\left(\frac{1}{N}\tilde{\mathbf{X}}\tilde{\mathbf{X}}^{\top}+\tilde{\gamma}{\mathbf{I}}_{p}\right)^{-1}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒘</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>N</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐑</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐗</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mi>𝐑</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>N</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐗</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐗</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>γ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐈</mi><mi>p</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\bm{w}}=\frac{1}{N}{\mathbf{R}}\tilde{\mathbf{X}}\tilde{\bm{y}},\quad{\mathbf{R}}=\left(\frac{1}{N}\tilde{\mathbf{X}}\tilde{\mathbf{X}}^{\top}+\tilde{\gamma}{\mathbf{I}}_{p}\right)^{-1}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Fine-tuning phase.</h4>

<div id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">During the fine-tuning phase, we suppose that we are given pairs of target data <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{({\bm{x}}_{i},y_{i})\}_{i=1}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{({\bm{x}}_{i},y_{i})\}_{i=1}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{i}\in\{-1,1\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{i}\in\{-1,1\}</annotation></semantics></math> that are distributed such that <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{X}}=\left[{\bm{x}}_{1},\ldots,{\bm{x}}_{n}\right]\in{\mathbb{R}}^{p\times n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐗</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{X}}=\left[{\bm{x}}_{1},\ldots,{\bm{x}}_{n}\right]\in{\mathbb{R}}^{p\times n}</annotation></semantics></math> is given by:</p>
<table id="S3.E6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{X}}={\bm{\mu}}_{\beta}{\bm{y}}^{\top}+{\mathbf{Z}},\quad{\bm{\mu}}_{\beta}=\beta{\bm{\mu}}+{\bm{\mu}}^{\perp}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐗</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝝁</mi><mi>β</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>𝐙</mi></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝝁</mi><mi>β</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝝁</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>𝝁</mi><mo>⟂</mo></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{X}}={\bm{\mu}}_{\beta}{\bm{y}}^{\top}+{\mathbf{Z}},\quad{\bm{\mu}}_{\beta}=\beta{\bm{\mu}}+{\bm{\mu}}^{\perp},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(6)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{Z}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐙</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{Z}}</annotation></semantics></math> is a random matrix with <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{N}}(0,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{N}}(0,1)</annotation></semantics></math> i.i.d. entries, <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{\mu}}^{\perp}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝝁</mi><mo>⟂</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{\mu}}^{\perp}</annotation></semantics></math> is an orthogonal vector to <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{\mu}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝝁</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{\mu}}</annotation></semantics></math> and the factor <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta\in{\mathbb{R}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta\in{\mathbb{R}}</annotation></semantics></math> quantifies the <span id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">alignment</span> between the source and target data, as we have that: <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\langle{\bm{\mu}}_{\beta},{\bm{\mu}}\rangle=\beta\|{\bm{\mu}}\|^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝝁</mi><mi>β</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>𝝁</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>𝝁</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\langle{\bm{\mu}}_{\beta},{\bm{\mu}}\rangle=\beta\|{\bm{\mu}}\|^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>. Leveraging the pre-trained weights <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\bm{w}}\in{\mathbb{R}}^{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒘</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>p</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\bm{w}}\in{\mathbb{R}}^{p}</annotation></semantics></math>, we consider the training of adapter weights <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{a}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒂</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{a}}</annotation></semantics></math> as:</p>
<table id="S3.E7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{a}}=\argmin_{\bm{v}}\frac{1}{n}\left\|{\mathbf{X}}^{\top}\left(\alpha\tilde{\bm{w}}+{\bm{v}}\right)-{\bm{y}}\right\|_{2}^{2}+\gamma\|{\bm{v}}\|_{2}^{2}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝒂</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>min</mi></mrow><mi>𝐯</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></mfrac></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>𝐗</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>𝐯</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>𝐲</mi></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>𝐯</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{a}}=\argmin_{\bm{v}}\frac{1}{n}\left\|{\mathbf{X}}^{\top}\left(\alpha\tilde{\bm{w}}+{\bm{v}}\right)-{\bm{y}}\right\|_{2}^{2}+\gamma\|{\bm{v}}\|_{2}^{2},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(7)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.3" class="ltx_p">for a scalar <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha\in{\mathbb{R}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha\in{\mathbb{R}}</annotation></semantics></math>. In fact, classical reparametrization approaches can be modeled by the same setting using <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=1</annotation></semantics></math>. Solving the previous minimization problem, <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{a}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒂</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{a}}</annotation></semantics></math> expresses as:</p>
<table id="S3.EGx2" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E8"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle{\bm{a}}=\frac{1}{n}\left(\frac{1}{n}{\mathbf{X}}{\mathbf{X}}^{\top}+\gamma{\mathbf{I}}_{p}\right)^{-1}\left({\mathbf{X}}{\bm{y}}-\alpha{\mathbf{X}}{\mathbf{X}}^{\top}\tilde{\bm{w}}\right)." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝒂</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝐗𝐗</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐈</mi><mi>p</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐗</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝒚</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝐗𝐗</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒘</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle{\bm{a}}=\frac{1}{n}\left(\frac{1}{n}{\mathbf{X}}{\mathbf{X}}^{\top}+\gamma{\mathbf{I}}_{p}\right)^{-1}\left({\mathbf{X}}{\bm{y}}-\alpha{\mathbf{X}}{\mathbf{X}}^{\top}\tilde{\bm{w}}\right).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(8)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.4" class="ltx_p">We define the resolvent matrices <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{Q}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐐</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{Q}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{R}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐑</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{R}}</annotation></semantics></math> by:</p>
<table id="S3.E9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{Q}}=\left(\frac{1}{n}{\mathbf{X}}{\mathbf{X}}^{\top}+\gamma{\mathbf{I}}_{p}\right)^{-1},\quad{\mathbf{R}}=\left(\frac{1}{N}\tilde{\mathbf{X}}\tilde{\mathbf{X}}^{\top}+\tilde{\gamma}{\mathbf{I}}_{p}\right)^{-1}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐐</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝐗𝐗</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐈</mi><mi>p</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mi>𝐑</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>N</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐗</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐗</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>γ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐈</mi><mi>p</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{Q}}=\left(\frac{1}{n}{\mathbf{X}}{\mathbf{X}}^{\top}+\gamma{\mathbf{I}}_{p}\right)^{-1},\quad{\mathbf{R}}=\left(\frac{1}{N}\tilde{\mathbf{X}}\tilde{\mathbf{X}}^{\top}+\tilde{\gamma}{\mathbf{I}}_{p}\right)^{-1},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(9)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.5" class="ltx_p">Then our obtained fine-tuned classifier <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{w}}_{\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒘</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{w}}_{\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math> writes:</p>
<table id="S3.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{w}}_{\alpha}=\alpha\tilde{\bm{w}}+{\bm{a}}=\frac{1}{n}{\mathbf{Q}}(\gamma){\mathbf{X}}{\bm{y}}+\alpha\gamma{\mathbf{Q}}\tilde{\bm{w}}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝒘</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒘</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>𝒂</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐐</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐗</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝒚</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐐</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒘</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{w}}_{\alpha}=\alpha\tilde{\bm{w}}+{\bm{a}}=\frac{1}{n}{\mathbf{Q}}(\gamma){\mathbf{X}}{\bm{y}}+\alpha\gamma{\mathbf{Q}}\tilde{\bm{w}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We denote by <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{w}}\equiv{\bm{w}}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝒘</mi><mo>≡</mo><msub><mi>𝒘</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{w}}\equiv{\bm{w}}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> the classifier obtained through learning directly on target data (without fine-tuning), which is given by:</p>
<table id="S3.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{w}}=\frac{1}{n}{\mathbf{Q}}(\gamma){\mathbf{X}}{\bm{y}}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝒘</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐐</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐗</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝒚</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{w}}=\frac{1}{n}{\mathbf{Q}}(\gamma){\mathbf{X}}{\bm{y}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(No-FT)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p2.2" class="ltx_p">Then we finally get the expression of our <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>-Fine-tuned classifier as follows:</p>
<table id="S3.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{w}}_{\alpha}={\bm{w}}+\alpha\gamma{\mathbf{Q}}\tilde{\bm{w}}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝒘</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>𝒘</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐐</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒘</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{w}}_{\alpha}={\bm{w}}+\alpha\gamma{\mathbf{Q}}\tilde{\bm{w}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(<math id="S3.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>-FTC)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 3.1</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(About the interpretability of our fine-tuned classifier)<span id="S3.Thmtheorem1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Remark that the parameter <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> introduced in the expression of the fine-tuned classifier <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{w}}_{\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐰</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{w}}_{\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math> characterizes the contribution of each training dataset (source and target) to the test performance on the target task. In fact, since the prediction of the class label does not change by multiplying <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{w}}_{\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐰</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{w}}_{\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math> by a positive constant, then by taking a positive <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> and for <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho=\frac{\alpha}{1+\alpha}\in(0,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mi>α</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho=\frac{\alpha}{1+\alpha}\in(0,1)</annotation></semantics></math>, the fine-tuned classifier is equivalent to this convex weighted classifier:</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{w}}_{\rho}=\rho\tilde{\bm{w}}+(1-\rho){\bm{a}}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝒘</mi><mi>ρ</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒘</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>ρ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝒂</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{w}}_{\rho}=\rho\tilde{\bm{w}}+(1-\rho){\bm{a}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">and therefore, this new parameter <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math> can be interpreted as the percentage of the contribution of the source task to the test performance on the target task.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 3.2</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(About the regularization parameter <math id="S3.Thmtheorem2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma</annotation></semantics></math>)<span id="S3.Thmtheorem2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">We remark from the expression of <math id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{w}}_{\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐰</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{w}}_{\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math> in equation <a href="#S3.Ex3" title="In Fine-tuning phase. ‣ 3.1 Theoretical Setting ‣ 3 Problem setting and Background ‣ 𝛼-LoRA: Effective Fine-Tuning via Base Model Rescaling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">
                  
                    α
                  
                -FTC</span></a> that the weight decay <math id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma</annotation></semantics></math> is essential to have the dependence of <math id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{w}}_{\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐰</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{w}}_{\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>. In fact, taking <math id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma\to 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma\to 0</annotation></semantics></math> leads to a fine-tuned classifier of the form:</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{w}}_{\alpha}=({\mathbf{X}}{\mathbf{X}}^{\top})^{+}{\mathbf{X}}{\bm{y}}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝒘</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝐗𝐗</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐗</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝒚</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{w}}_{\alpha}=({\mathbf{X}}{\mathbf{X}}^{\top})^{+}{\mathbf{X}}{\bm{y}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where <math id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({\mathbf{X}}{\mathbf{X}}^{\top})^{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝐗𝐗</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\mathbf{X}}{\mathbf{X}}^{\top})^{+}</annotation></semantics></math> is the Moore-Penrose inverse of the symmetric semi-definite matrix <math id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{X}}{\mathbf{X}}^{\top}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐗𝐗</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{X}}{\mathbf{X}}^{\top}</annotation></semantics></math>. Therefore, the obtained classifier does <span id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">not</span> depend on <math id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> here, nor on the pre-trained model <math id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\bm{w}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\bm{w}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Additionally, having such a regularization technique is essential in transfer learning since the target dataset is generally much smaller than the pre-training one, and therefore the fine-tuning process can easily lead to overfitting in the absence of a regularization technique.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2 </span>RMT Background</h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To analyze the performance of the fine-tuned classifier <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{w}}_{\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒘</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{w}}_{\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math>, we can leverage tools from Random Matrix Theory. In mathematical terms, the understanding of the asymptotic performance of the classifier <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{w}}_{\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒘</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{w}}_{\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math> boils down to the characterization of the statistical behavior of the <span id="S3.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">resolvent matrices</span> <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{Q}}(z)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐐</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{Q}}(z)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{R}}(z)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐑</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{R}}(z)</annotation></semantics></math> introduced in equation <a href="#S3.E9" title="In Fine-tuning phase. ‣ 3.1 Theoretical Setting ‣ 3 Problem setting and Background ‣ 𝛼-LoRA: Effective Fine-Tuning via Base Model Rescaling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>. In the following, we will recall some important notions and results from random matrix theory, which will be at the heart of our analysis. We start by defining the main object, which is the resolvent matrix.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 3.3</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Resolvent)<span id="S3.Thmtheorem3.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For a symmetric matrix <math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{M}}\in{\mathbb{R}}^{p\times p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐌</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{M}}\in{\mathbb{R}}^{p\times p}</annotation></semantics></math>, the resolvent <math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{Q}}_{M}(z)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐐</mi><mi>M</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{Q}}_{M}(z)</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{M}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐌</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{M}}</annotation></semantics></math> is defined for <math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z\in{\mathbb{C}}\backslash{\mathcal{S}}({\mathbf{M}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">\</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐌</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z\in{\mathbb{C}}\backslash{\mathcal{S}}({\mathbf{M}})</annotation></semantics></math> as:</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{Q}}_{M}(z)=({\mathbf{M}}-z{\mathbf{I}}_{p})^{-1}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝐐</mi><mi>M</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>𝐌</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐈</mi><mi>p</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{Q}}_{M}(z)=({\mathbf{M}}-z{\mathbf{I}}_{p})^{-1},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where <math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{S}}({\mathbf{M}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐌</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{S}}({\mathbf{M}})</annotation></semantics></math> is the set of eigenvalues or spectrum of <math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{M}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐌</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{M}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In fact, the study of the asymptotic performance of <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{w}}_{\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒘</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{w}}_{\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math> involves the estimation of linear forms of the resolvents <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{Q}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐐</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{Q}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{R}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐑</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{R}}</annotation></semantics></math> in equation <a href="#S3.E9" title="In Fine-tuning phase. ‣ 3.1 Theoretical Setting ‣ 3 Problem setting and Background ‣ 𝛼-LoRA: Effective Fine-Tuning via Base Model Rescaling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>, such as <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{1}{n}\Tr{\mathbf{Q}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>Tr</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>𝐐</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{1}{n}\Tr{\mathbf{Q}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{a}}^{\top}{\mathbf{Q}}{\bm{b}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝒂</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐐</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝒃</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{a}}^{\top}{\mathbf{Q}}{\bm{b}}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{a}},{\bm{b}}\in{\mathbb{R}}^{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝒂</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝒃</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>p</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{a}},{\bm{b}}\in{\mathbb{R}}^{p}</annotation></semantics></math> of bounded Euclidean norms. Therefore, the notion of a <span id="S3.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">deterministic equivalent</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hachem et al., 2007</a>)</cite> is crucial as it allows the design of a <span id="S3.SS2.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">deterministic</span> matrix, having (in probability or almost surely) asymptotically the same <span id="S3.SS2.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">scalar observations</span> as the random ones in the sense of <span id="S3.SS2.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">linear forms</span>.
A rigorous definition is provided below.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 3.4</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Deterministic equivalent <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hachem et al., 2007</a>)</cite>)<span id="S3.Thmtheorem4.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">We say that <math id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{\mathbf{Q}}\in{\mathbb{R}}^{p\times p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐐</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{\mathbf{Q}}\in{\mathbb{R}}^{p\times p}</annotation></semantics></math> is a deterministic equivalent for the random resolvent matrix <math id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{Q}}\in{\mathbb{R}}^{p\times p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐐</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{Q}}\in{\mathbb{R}}^{p\times p}</annotation></semantics></math> if, for any bounded linear form <math id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u:{\mathbb{R}}^{p\times p}\to{\mathbb{R}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u:{\mathbb{R}}^{p\times p}\to{\mathbb{R}}</annotation></semantics></math>, we have that, as <math id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p\to\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p\to\infty</annotation></semantics></math>:</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u({\mathbf{Q}})\asto u(\bar{\mathbf{Q}})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐐</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic" mathsize="0.700em">a.s.</mtext></mover><mi>u</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐐</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u({\mathbf{Q}})\asto u(\bar{\mathbf{Q}}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where the convergence is in the almost sure sense.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">In particular, a deterministic equivalent for the resolvents <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{Q}}(z)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐐</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{Q}}(z)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{R}}(z)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐑</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{R}}(z)</annotation></semantics></math> defined in equation <a href="#S3.E9" title="In Fine-tuning phase. ‣ 3.1 Theoretical Setting ‣ 3 Problem setting and Background ‣ 𝛼-LoRA: Effective Fine-Tuning via Base Model Rescaling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a> is given by the following Lemma (the proof is presented in Appendix <a href="#A1.SS2" title="A.2 Deterministic equivalents ‣ Appendix A Useful results ‣ 𝛼-LoRA: Effective Fine-Tuning via Base Model Rescaling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.2</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem5" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 3.5</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Deterministic equivalent of <math id="S3.Thmtheorem5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{Q}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐐</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{Q}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.Thmtheorem5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{R}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐑</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{R}}</annotation></semantics></math>)<span id="S3.Thmtheorem5.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem5.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Under the high-dimensional regime, when <math id="S3.Thmtheorem5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p,n,N\to\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p,n,N\to\infty</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S3.Thmtheorem5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{p}{n}\to\eta\in(0,\infty)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mi>p</mi><mi>n</mi></mfrac><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>η</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{p}{n}\to\eta\in(0,\infty)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.Thmtheorem5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{p}{N}\to\tilde{\eta}\in(0,\infty)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mi>p</mi><mi>N</mi></mfrac><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>η</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{p}{N}\to\tilde{\eta}\in(0,\infty)</annotation></semantics></math> and assuming <math id="S3.Thmtheorem5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|{\bm{\mu}}\|={\mathcal{O}}(1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>𝛍</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|{\bm{\mu}}\|={\mathcal{O}}(1)</annotation></semantics></math>, a deterministic equivalent for <math id="S3.Thmtheorem5.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{Q}}\equiv{\mathbf{Q}}(\gamma)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐐</mi><mo>≡</mo><mrow><mi>𝐐</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{Q}}\equiv{\mathbf{Q}}(\gamma)</annotation></semantics></math> and for <math id="S3.Thmtheorem5.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{R}}\equiv{\mathbf{R}}(\gamma)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐑</mi><mo>≡</mo><mrow><mi>𝐑</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{R}}\equiv{\mathbf{R}}(\gamma)</annotation></semantics></math>, previously defined in equation <a href="#S3.E9" title="In Fine-tuning phase. ‣ 3.1 Theoretical Setting ‣ 3 Problem setting and Background ‣ 𝛼-LoRA: Effective Fine-Tuning via Base Model Rescaling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>, denoted <math id="S3.Thmtheorem5.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{\mathbf{Q}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐐</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{\mathbf{Q}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.Thmtheorem5.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{\mathbf{R}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐑</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{\mathbf{R}}</annotation></semantics></math> respectively, are given by:</span></p>
<table id="S3.EGx3" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.Ex8"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\bar{\mathbf{Q}}(\gamma)=\left(\frac{{\bm{\mu}}_{\beta}{\bm{\mu}}_{\beta}^{\top}+{\mathbf{I}}_{p}}{1+\delta_{Q}}+\gamma{\mathbf{I}}_{p}\right)^{-1},\quad\bar{\mathbf{R}}(\gamma)=\left(\frac{{\bm{\mu}}{\bm{\mu}}^{\top}+{\mathbf{I}}_{p}}{1+\delta_{R}}+\gamma{\mathbf{I}}_{p}\right)^{-1}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐐</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝝁</mi><mi>β</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>𝝁</mi><mi>β</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>𝐈</mi><mi>p</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐈</mi><mi>p</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐑</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mi>𝝁</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝝁</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>𝐈</mi><mi>p</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>R</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐈</mi><mi>p</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\bar{\mathbf{Q}}(\gamma)=\left(\frac{{\bm{\mu}}_{\beta}{\bm{\mu}}_{\beta}^{\top}+{\mathbf{I}}_{p}}{1+\delta_{Q}}+\gamma{\mathbf{I}}_{p}\right)^{-1},\quad\bar{\mathbf{R}}(\gamma)=\left(\frac{{\bm{\mu}}{\bm{\mu}}^{\top}+{\mathbf{I}}_{p}}{1+\delta_{R}}+\gamma{\mathbf{I}}_{p}\right)^{-1}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem5.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem5.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Where:</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{Q}=\frac{1}{n}\Tr\bar{\mathbf{Q}}=\frac{\eta-\gamma-1+\sqrt{(\eta-\gamma-1)^{2}+4\eta\gamma}}{2\gamma},\quad\delta_{R}=\frac{\tilde{\eta}-\tilde{\gamma}-1+\sqrt{(\tilde{\eta}-\tilde{\gamma}-1)^{2}+4\tilde{\eta}\tilde{\gamma}}}{2\tilde{\gamma}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>Tr</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐐</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>+</mo><msqrt><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>η</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>γ</mi></mrow></mrow></msqrt></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>γ</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>R</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>η</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>−</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>γ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>+</mo><msqrt><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>η</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>−</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>γ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>η</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>γ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow></mrow></msqrt></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>γ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{Q}=\frac{1}{n}\Tr\bar{\mathbf{Q}}=\frac{\eta-\gamma-1+\sqrt{(\eta-\gamma-1)^{2}+4\eta\gamma}}{2\gamma},\quad\delta_{R}=\frac{\tilde{\eta}-\tilde{\gamma}-1+\sqrt{(\tilde{\eta}-\tilde{\gamma}-1)^{2}+4\tilde{\eta}\tilde{\gamma}}}{2\tilde{\gamma}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Main results</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">After having defined the setting and needed background, we will now present our main technical results, which describe the asymptotic behavior of the fine-tuned classifier defined in equation <a href="#S3.Ex3" title="In Fine-tuning phase. ‣ 3.1 Theoretical Setting ‣ 3 Problem setting and Background ‣ 𝛼-LoRA: Effective Fine-Tuning via Base Model Rescaling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">
                  
                    α
                  
                -FTC</span></a>. Specifically, we provide our results under the following growth rate assumptions (classical assumptions in Random Matrix Theory).</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_assumption">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Assumption 4.1</span></span><span id="S4.Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Growth Rates)<span id="S4.Thmtheorem1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Suppose that as <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p,n,N\to\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p,n,N\to\infty</annotation></semantics></math>:</span></p>
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.2" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">1) </span><math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{p}{n}\to\eta\in[0,\infty)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mi>p</mi><mi>n</mi></mfrac><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>η</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{p}{n}\to\eta\in[0,\infty)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">,  
2) </span><math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{p}{N}\to\tilde{\eta}\in[0,\infty)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mi>p</mi><mi>N</mi></mfrac><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>η</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{p}{N}\to\tilde{\eta}\in[0,\infty)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">,  
3) </span><math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|{\bm{\mu}}\|={\mathcal{O}}(1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>𝝁</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|{\bm{\mu}}\|={\mathcal{O}}(1)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">,   
4) </span><math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|{\bm{\mu}}_{\beta}\|={\mathcal{O}}(1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi>𝝁</mi><mi>β</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|{\bm{\mu}}_{\beta}\|={\mathcal{O}}(1)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.2.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The first and second assumptions simply state that our analysis considers both the low (<math id="S4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta,\tilde{\eta}\ll 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>η</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow><mo>≪</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta,\tilde{\eta}\ll 1</annotation></semantics></math>) and high (<math id="S4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta,\tilde{\eta}\gg 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>η</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow><mo>≫</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta,\tilde{\eta}\gg 1</annotation></semantics></math>) dimensional regimes. The third and last assumptions are also fundamental and state that the norm of the source <math id="S4.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{\mu}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝝁</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{\mu}}</annotation></semantics></math> and target <math id="S4.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{\mu}}_{\beta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝝁</mi><mi>β</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{\mu}}_{\beta}</annotation></semantics></math> data means do not scale with the dimension <math id="S4.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>, which makes the classification problem neither easy (<math id="S4.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|{\bm{\mu}}\|\to\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>𝝁</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|{\bm{\mu}}\|\to\infty</annotation></semantics></math>) nor impossible (<math id="S4.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|{\bm{\mu}}\|\to 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>𝝁</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|{\bm{\mu}}\|\to 0</annotation></semantics></math>) in high dimensions. Having stated the main assumptions, we are now in a position to present our main technical findings about the theoretical test performance of the fine-tuned classifier <a href="#S3.Ex3" title="In Fine-tuning phase. ‣ 3.1 Theoretical Setting ‣ 3 Problem setting and Background ‣ 𝛼-LoRA: Effective Fine-Tuning via Base Model Rescaling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">
                  
                    α
                  
                -FTC</span></a>. But beforehand, let us define some scalar quantities that will be useful in our derivations:</p>
<table id="S4.EGx4" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.Ex10"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\lambda_{Q}=\|{\bm{\mu}}_{\beta}\|^{2}+1+\gamma(1+\delta_{Q}),\quad" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi>𝝁</mi><mi>β</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\lambda_{Q}=\|{\bm{\mu}}_{\beta}\|^{2}+1+\gamma(1+\delta_{Q}),\quad</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\lambda_{R}=\|{\bm{\mu}}\|^{2}+1+\tilde{\gamma}(1+\delta_{R}),\quad h=1-\frac{\eta}{(1+\gamma(1+\delta_{Q}))^{2}}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>R</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>𝝁</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>γ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>R</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mi>η</mi><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\lambda_{R}=\|{\bm{\mu}}\|^{2}+1+\tilde{\gamma}(1+\delta_{R}),\quad h=1-\frac{\eta}{(1+\gamma(1+\delta_{Q}))^{2}},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S4.Ex11"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\tilde{h}=1-\frac{\tilde{\eta}}{(1+\tilde{\gamma}(1+\delta_{R}))^{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>h</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mover accent="true"><mi>η</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>γ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>R</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\tilde{h}=1-\frac{\tilde{\eta}}{(1+\tilde{\gamma}(1+\delta_{R}))^{2}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.p2.2" class="ltx_p">Our main theorem below describes the behavior of the decision function of our fine-tuned classifier.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S4.p3.pic1" class="ltx_picture" height="183.9" overflow="visible" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 550 183.9" width="550"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,183.9) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0)"><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#BF0000;" fill="#BF0000" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0 8.3 L 0 175.6 C 0 180.19 3.72 183.9 8.3 183.9 L 541.7 183.9 C 546.28 183.9 550 180.19 550 175.6 L 550 8.3 C 550 3.72 546.28 0 541.7 0 L 8.3 0 C 3.72 0 0 3.72 0 8.3 Z"></path></g><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#FFF2F2;" fill="#FFF2F2" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 1.38 8.3 L 1.38 175.6 C 1.38 179.42 4.48 182.52 8.3 182.52 L 541.7 182.52 C 545.52 182.52 548.62 179.42 548.62 175.6 L 548.62 8.3 C 548.62 4.48 545.52 1.38 541.7 1.38 L 8.3 1.38 C 4.48 1.38 1.38 4.48 1.38 8.3 Z"></path></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 21.07 161.1)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:36.7em;--ltx-fo-height:0.69em;--ltx-fo-depth:10.69em;font-size:10pt;" height="157.52" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 9.61)" width="507.82"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><span id="S4.p3.pic1.1" class="ltx_inline-logical-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_bottom" style="width:36.7em;">
<span id="S4.Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 4.2</span></span><span id="S4.Thmtheorem2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Gaussianity of the fine-tuned Ridge model)<span id="S4.Thmtheorem2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<span id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<span id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Let <math id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{w}}_{\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝐰</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">α</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{w}}_{\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math> be the fine-tuned classifier as defined in equation <a href="#S3.Ex3" title="In Fine-tuning phase. ‣ 3.1 Theoretical Setting ‣ 3 Problem setting and Background ‣ 𝛼-LoRA: Effective Fine-Tuning via Base Model Rescaling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">
                  
                    α
                  
                -FTC</span></a> and suppose that Assumption <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem1" title="Assumption 4.1 (Growth Rates). ‣ 4 Main results ‣ 𝛼-LoRA: Effective Fine-Tuning via Base Model Rescaling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a> holds. The decision function <math id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{w}}_{\alpha}^{\top}{\bm{x}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝐰</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">α</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝐱</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{w}}_{\alpha}^{\top}{\bm{x}}</annotation></semantics></math>, on some test sample <math id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{x}}\in{\mathcal{C}}_{a}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝐱</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">∈</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathcolor="#000000">𝒞</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">a</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{x}}\in{\mathcal{C}}_{a}</annotation></semantics></math> independent of <math id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{X}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝐗</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{X}}</annotation></semantics></math>, satisfies:</span></span>
<span id="S4.EGx5" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<span id="S4.Ex12"><span class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<span class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></span>
<span class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle{\bm{w}}_{\alpha}^{\top}{\bm{x}}\,\,\toind\,\,{\mathcal{N}}\left((-1)^{a}m_{\alpha},\,\nu_{\alpha}-m_{\alpha}^{2}\right)," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝒘</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">α</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝒙</mi><mo lspace="0.618em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">→</mo><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathcolor="#000000" mathsize="0.700em">𝒟</mo></mover><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathcolor="#000000">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">(</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">−</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">a</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">m</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">α</mi></msub></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">ν</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">α</mi></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">−</mo><msubsup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">m</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">α</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle{\bm{w}}_{\alpha}^{\top}{\bm{x}}\,\,\toind\,\,{\mathcal{N}}\left((-1)^{a}m_{\alpha},\,\nu_{\alpha}-m_{\alpha}^{2}\right),</annotation></semantics></math></span>
<span class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></span></span></span>
</span>
</span>
<span id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<span id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">where:</span></span>
<span id="S4.EGx6" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<span id="S4.Ex13"><span class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<span class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></span>
<span class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle m_{\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">m</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">α</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle m_{\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math></span>
<span class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex13.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\frac{1}{\lambda_{Q}}\left(\|{\bm{\mu}}_{\beta}\|^{2}+\frac{\alpha\beta\gamma(1+\delta_{Q})}{\lambda_{R}}\|{\bm{\mu}}\|^{2}\right)," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000"><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">λ</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">Q</mi></msub></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">(</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝝁</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">β</mi></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">2</mn></msup><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">+</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000"><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">α</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">β</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">γ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">+</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">δ</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">Q</mi></msub></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">λ</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">R</mi></msub></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝝁</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\frac{1}{\lambda_{Q}}\left(\|{\bm{\mu}}_{\beta}\|^{2}+\frac{\alpha\beta\gamma(1+\delta_{Q})}{\lambda_{R}}\|{\bm{\mu}}\|^{2}\right),</annotation></semantics></math></span>
<span class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></span></span></span>
<span id="S4.Ex14"><span class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<span class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></span>
<span class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\nu_{\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">ν</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">α</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\nu_{\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math></span>
<span class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex14.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=T_{1}+\alpha T_{2}+\alpha^{2}T_{3}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">T</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">+</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">α</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">T</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">2</mn></msub></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">+</mo><mrow><msup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">α</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">T</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">3</mn></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" lspace="0em" mathcolor="#000000">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=T_{1}+\alpha T_{2}+\alpha^{2}T_{3}.</annotation></semantics></math></span>
<span class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></span></span></span>
</span>
<span id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p2.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p2.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">With:</span></span>
<span id="S4.EGx7" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<span id="S4.Ex15"><span class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<span class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></span>
<span class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></span>
<span class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle T_{1}=\frac{\|{\bm{\mu}}_{\beta}\|^{2}}{h\lambda_{Q}}\left(\frac{\|{\bm{\mu}}_{\beta}\|^{2}+1}{\lambda_{Q}}-2(1-h)\right)+\frac{1-h}{h}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">T</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000"><msup><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝝁</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">β</mi></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">2</mn></msup><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">λ</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">Q</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">(</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000"><mrow><msup><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝝁</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">β</mi></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">2</mn></msup><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">+</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn></mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">λ</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">Q</mi></msub></mfrac></mstyle><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">−</mo><mrow><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">−</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">h</mi></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">+</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000"><mrow><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">−</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">h</mi></mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">h</mi></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle T_{1}=\frac{\|{\bm{\mu}}_{\beta}\|^{2}}{h\lambda_{Q}}\left(\frac{\|{\bm{\mu}}_{\beta}\|^{2}+1}{\lambda_{Q}}-2(1-h)\right)+\frac{1-h}{h},</annotation></semantics></math></span>
<span class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></span></span></span>
<span id="S4.Ex16"><span class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<span class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></span>
<span class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></span>
<span class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle T_{2}=\frac{2\gamma\beta(1+\delta_{Q})\|{\bm{\mu}}\|^{2}}{\lambda_{R}\lambda_{Q}}\left(1-\frac{\gamma(1+\delta_{Q})}{h\lambda_{Q}}\right)," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">T</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">2</mn></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000"><mrow><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">γ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">β</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">+</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">δ</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">Q</mi></msub></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝝁</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">2</mn></msup></mrow><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">λ</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">R</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">λ</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">Q</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">(</mo><mrow><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">−</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000"><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">+</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">δ</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">Q</mi></msub></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">λ</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">Q</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle T_{2}=\frac{2\gamma\beta(1+\delta_{Q})\|{\bm{\mu}}\|^{2}}{\lambda_{R}\lambda_{Q}}\left(1-\frac{\gamma(1+\delta_{Q})}{h\lambda_{Q}}\right),</annotation></semantics></math></span>
<span class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></span></span></span>
<span id="S4.Ex17"><span class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<span class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></span>
<span class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></span>
<span class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex17.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle T_{3}=\frac{\gamma^{2}(1+\delta_{Q})^{2}}{h}\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">T</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">3</mn></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000"><mrow><msup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">γ</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">+</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">δ</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">Q</mi></msub></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">2</mn></msup></mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">h</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" lspace="0.222em" mathcolor="#000000">×</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle T_{3}=\frac{\gamma^{2}(1+\delta_{Q})^{2}}{h}\times</annotation></semantics></math></span>
<span class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></span></span></span>
<span id="S4.Ex18"><span class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<span class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></span>
<span class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></span>
<span class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\left[\frac{\|{\bm{\mu}}\|^{2}}{\lambda_{R}^{2}}\left(\frac{\beta^{2}\|{\bm{\mu}}\|^{2}}{\lambda_{Q}^{2}}+\frac{1-h}{\eta}\left(1+\frac{\beta^{2}\|{\bm{\mu}}\|^{2}\|{\bm{\mu}}_{\beta}\|^{2}}{\lambda_{Q}^{2}}-\frac{2\beta^{2}\|{\bm{\mu}}\|^{2}}{\lambda_{Q}}+(1-\tilde{h})\left(1-\frac{2\|{\bm{\mu}}\|^{2}}{\lambda_{R}}\right)\right)\right)\right]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">[</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000"><msup><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝝁</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">2</mn></msup><msubsup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">λ</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">R</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">2</mn></msubsup></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">(</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000"><mrow><msup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">β</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝝁</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">2</mn></msup></mrow><msubsup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">λ</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">Q</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">2</mn></msubsup></mfrac></mstyle><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">+</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000"><mrow><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">−</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">h</mi></mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">η</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">+</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000"><mrow><msup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">β</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝝁</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝝁</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">β</mi></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">2</mn></msup></mrow><msubsup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">λ</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">Q</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">2</mn></msubsup></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">−</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000"><mrow><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">β</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝝁</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">2</mn></msup></mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">λ</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">Q</mi></msub></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">−</mo><mover accent="true"><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">h</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">~</mo></mover></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">(</mo><mrow><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">−</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000"><mrow><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝝁</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">2</mn></msup></mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">λ</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">R</mi></msub></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\left[\frac{\|{\bm{\mu}}\|^{2}}{\lambda_{R}^{2}}\left(\frac{\beta^{2}\|{\bm{\mu}}\|^{2}}{\lambda_{Q}^{2}}+\frac{1-h}{\eta}\left(1+\frac{\beta^{2}\|{\bm{\mu}}\|^{2}\|{\bm{\mu}}_{\beta}\|^{2}}{\lambda_{Q}^{2}}-\frac{2\beta^{2}\|{\bm{\mu}}\|^{2}}{\lambda_{Q}}+(1-\tilde{h})\left(1-\frac{2\|{\bm{\mu}}\|^{2}}{\lambda_{R}}\right)\right)\right)\right]</annotation></semantics></math></span>
<span class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></span></span></span>
</span>
</span>
</span></span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span>
</div>
<div id="S4.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.p4.1" class="ltx_p">In simple terms, Theorem <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem2" title="Theorem 4.2 (Gaussianity of the fine-tuned Ridge model). ‣ 4 Main results ‣ 𝛼-LoRA: Effective Fine-Tuning via Base Model Rescaling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.2</span></a> states that the decision function of the classifier in equation <a href="#S3.Ex3" title="In Fine-tuning phase. ‣ 3.1 Theoretical Setting ‣ 3 Problem setting and Background ‣ 𝛼-LoRA: Effective Fine-Tuning via Base Model Rescaling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">
                  
                    α
                  
                -FTC</span></a> is asymptotically equivalent to the thresholding of two monovariate Gaussian random variables with respective means <math id="S4.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m_{\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-m_{\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>α</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-m_{\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math> and standard deviation <math id="S4.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu_{\alpha}-m_{\alpha}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ν</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>m</mi><mi>α</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu_{\alpha}-m_{\alpha}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, where the statistics <math id="S4.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m_{\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu_{\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ν</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu_{\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math> are expressed in terms of the scalar quantities defined above (see Figure <a href="#A2.F6" title="Figure 6 ‣ Terms in 𝛼^2 ‣ B.2 Test Variance ‣ Appendix B RMT Analysis of the fine-tuned classifier ‣ 𝛼-LoRA: Effective Fine-Tuning via Base Model Rescaling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a> in the Appendix). Having characterized the distribution of the decision function of <math id="S4.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{w}}_{\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒘</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{w}}_{\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math>, we can now estimate its generalization performance, such as its test accuracy.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F1" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.21345v1/optimize-N-5000-n-40-p-1000-mu-0.8-mu_orth-0.8-gamma_pre-5-gamma_ft-0.1.svg" id="S4.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_figure_panel ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:548/135;" width="548" height="135"></object></div><div class="ltx_flex_break"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.21345v1/legend_4.svg" id="S4.F1.g2" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_figure_panel ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:411/22;" width="411" height="22"></object></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 1: </span>Theoretical Test Accuracy variation with <math id="S4.F1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S4.F1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N=5000" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>5000</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N=5000</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.F1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=40" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>40</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=40</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.F1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p=1000" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1000</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p=1000</annotation></semantics></math>, and the theoretical model is modified to take <math id="S4.F1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S4.F1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(0,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(0,1)</annotation></semantics></math>: <math id="S4.F1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{\mu}}_{\beta}=\beta{\bm{\mu}}+\sqrt{1-\beta^{2}}{\bm{\mu}}^{\perp}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝝁</mi><mi>β</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝝁</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>β</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></msqrt><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝝁</mi><mo>⟂</mo></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{\mu}}_{\beta}=\beta{\bm{\mu}}+\sqrt{1-\beta^{2}}{\bm{\mu}}^{\perp}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S4.F1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|{\bm{\mu}}\|=\|{\bm{\mu}}^{\perp}\|=0.8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>𝝁</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msup><mi>𝝁</mi><mo>⟂</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0.8</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|{\bm{\mu}}\|=\|{\bm{\mu}}^{\perp}\|=0.8</annotation></semantics></math>. Finally the regularization parameters are: <math id="S4.F1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\gamma}=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>γ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\gamma}=2</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.F1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma=10^{-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma=10^{-1}</annotation></semantics></math>. </figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 4.3</span></span><span id="S4.Thmtheorem3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Asymptotic test accuracy of <math id="S4.Thmtheorem3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{w}}_{\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒘</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{w}}_{\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math>)<span id="S4.Thmtheorem3.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The asymptotic test accuracy of <math id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{w}}_{\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐰</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{w}}_{\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math> defined in equation <a href="#S3.Ex3" title="In Fine-tuning phase. ‣ 3.1 Theoretical Setting ‣ 3 Problem setting and Background ‣ 𝛼-LoRA: Effective Fine-Tuning via Base Model Rescaling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">
                  
                    α
                  
                -FTC</span></a>, under Assumption <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem1" title="Assumption 4.1 (Growth Rates). ‣ 4 Main results ‣ 𝛼-LoRA: Effective Fine-Tuning via Base Model Rescaling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a>, and as the number of test samples <math id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{\text{test}}\to\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">test</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{\text{test}}\to\infty</annotation></semantics></math>, is given by:</span></p>
<table id="S4.EGx8" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.Ex19"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex19.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle{\mathcal{A}}_{\text{test}}\asto 1-\varphi\left((\nu_{\alpha}-m_{\alpha}^{2})^{-\frac{1}{2}}m_{\alpha}\right),\quad\text{where: }\varphi(x)=\frac{1}{\sqrt{2\pi}}\int_{x}^{+\infty}e^{-\frac{t^{2}}{2}}\mathrm{dt}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">test</mtext></msub><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic" mathsize="0.700em">a.s.</mtext></mover><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>φ</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ν</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>m</mi><mi>α</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow></msup><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">where: </mtext><mi>φ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>π</mi></mrow></msqrt></mfrac></mstyle><mstyle displaystyle="true"><msubsup><mo>∫</mo><mi>x</mi><mrow><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow></msubsup></mstyle><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mfrac><msup><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow></msup><mi>dt</mi><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle{\mathcal{A}}_{\text{test}}\asto 1-\varphi\left((\nu_{\alpha}-m_{\alpha}^{2})^{-\frac{1}{2}}m_{\alpha}\right),\quad\text{where: }\varphi(x)=\frac{1}{\sqrt{2\pi}}\int_{x}^{+\infty}e^{-\frac{t^{2}}{2}}\mathrm{dt}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Therefore, thanks to Proposition <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem3" title="Proposition 4.3 (Asymptotic test accuracy of 𝒘_𝛼). ‣ 4 Main results ‣ 𝛼-LoRA: Effective Fine-Tuning via Base Model Rescaling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.3</span></a>, we now have the exact formulas of the theoretical test accuracy of our classifier <math id="S4.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{w}}_{\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒘</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{w}}_{\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math>, which can be used to characterize the expression of the optimal/worst parameters of the model (for instance, the <math id="S4.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>) to use for the fine-tuning process. In particular, we will derive the theoretical expressions of the extremum of <math id="S4.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> that lead to either the best or the worst test accuracy on the target task (proof in Appendix <a href="#A2" title="Appendix B RMT Analysis of the fine-tuned classifier ‣ 𝛼-LoRA: Effective Fine-Tuning via Base Model Rescaling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S4.p6.pic1" class="ltx_picture" height="139.58" overflow="visible" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 550 139.58" width="550"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,139.58) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0)"><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#BF0000;" fill="#BF0000" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0 8.3 L 0 131.28 C 0 135.87 3.72 139.58 8.3 139.58 L 541.7 139.58 C 546.28 139.58 550 135.87 550 131.28 L 550 8.3 C 550 3.72 546.28 0 541.7 0 L 8.3 0 C 3.72 0 0 3.72 0 8.3 Z"></path></g><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#FFF2F2;" fill="#FFF2F2" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 1.38 8.3 L 1.38 131.28 C 1.38 135.1 4.48 138.2 8.3 138.2 L 541.7 138.2 C 545.52 138.2 548.62 135.1 548.62 131.28 L 548.62 8.3 C 548.62 4.48 545.52 1.38 541.7 1.38 L 8.3 1.38 C 4.48 1.38 1.38 4.48 1.38 8.3 Z"></path></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 21.07 116.78)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:36.7em;--ltx-fo-height:0.69em;--ltx-fo-depth:7.49em;font-size:10pt;" height="113.2" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 9.61)" width="507.82"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><span id="S4.p6.pic1.1" class="ltx_inline-logical-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_bottom" style="width:36.7em;">
<span id="S4.Thmtheorem4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 4.4</span></span><span id="S4.Thmtheorem4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Optimal <math id="S4.Thmtheorem4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>)<span id="S4.Thmtheorem4.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<span id="S4.Thmtheorem4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<span id="S4.Thmtheorem4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Maximizing the term <math id="S4.Thmtheorem4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left((\nu_{\alpha}-m_{\alpha}^{2})^{-\frac{1}{2}}m_{\alpha}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">(</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">ν</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">α</mi></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">−</mo><msubsup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">m</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">α</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">−</mo><mfrac style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000"><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">2</mn></mfrac></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">m</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">α</mi></msub></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left((\nu_{\alpha}-m_{\alpha}^{2})^{-\frac{1}{2}}m_{\alpha}\right)</annotation></semantics></math> in terms of <math id="S4.Thmtheorem4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> leads to maximizing the test accuracy <math id="S4.Thmtheorem4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{A}}_{\text{test}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathcolor="#000000">𝒜</mi><mtext style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" class="ltx_mathvariant_italic" mathcolor="#000000">test</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{A}}_{\text{test}}</annotation></semantics></math>, and gives a unique maximizer <math id="S4.Thmtheorem4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha^{\star}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">α</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">⋆</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha^{\star}</annotation></semantics></math> given by:</span></span>
<span id="S4.Ex20" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<span><span class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<span class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></span>
<span class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex20.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha^{\star}=\frac{\lambda_{R}T_{2}\|{\bm{\mu}}_{\beta}\|^{2}-2\beta\gamma T_{1}(1+\delta_{Q})\|{\bm{\mu}}\|^{2}}{\beta\gamma T_{2}(1+\delta_{Q})\|{\bm{\mu}}\|^{2}-2\lambda_{R}T_{3}\|{\bm{\mu}}_{\beta}\|^{2}}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">α</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">⋆</mo></msup><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><mfrac style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000"><mrow><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">λ</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">R</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">T</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝝁</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">β</mi></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">2</mn></msup></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">−</mo><mrow><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">β</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">γ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">T</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">+</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">δ</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">Q</mi></msub></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝝁</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">β</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">γ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">T</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">+</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">δ</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">Q</mi></msub></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝝁</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">2</mn></msup></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">−</mo><mrow><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">λ</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">R</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">T</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">3</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝝁</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">β</mi></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha^{\star}=\frac{\lambda_{R}T_{2}\|{\bm{\mu}}_{\beta}\|^{2}-2\beta\gamma T_{1}(1+\delta_{Q})\|{\bm{\mu}}\|^{2}}{\beta\gamma T_{2}(1+\delta_{Q})\|{\bm{\mu}}\|^{2}-2\lambda_{R}T_{3}\|{\bm{\mu}}_{\beta}\|^{2}}</annotation></semantics></math></span>
<span class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></span></span></span>
</span>
<span id="S4.Thmtheorem4.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem4.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Plus, solving <math id="S4.Thmtheorem4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\nu_{\alpha}-m_{\alpha}^{2})^{-\frac{1}{2}}m_{\alpha}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">ν</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">α</mi></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">−</mo><msubsup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">m</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">α</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">−</mo><mfrac style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000"><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">2</mn></mfrac></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">m</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">α</mi></msub></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\nu_{\alpha}-m_{\alpha}^{2})^{-\frac{1}{2}}m_{\alpha}=0</annotation></semantics></math> leads to the unique minimizer <math id="S4.Thmtheorem4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">α</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S4.Thmtheorem4.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{A}}_{\text{test}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathcolor="#000000">𝒜</mi><mtext style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" class="ltx_mathvariant_italic" mathcolor="#000000">test</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{A}}_{\text{test}}</annotation></semantics></math>, which is given by:</span></span>
<span id="S4.Ex21" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<span><span class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<span class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></span>
<span class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex21.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{\alpha}=-\frac{\lambda_{R}\|{\bm{\mu}}_{\beta}\|^{2}}{\beta\gamma(1+\delta_{Q})\|{\bm{\mu}}\|^{2}}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">α</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">¯</mo></mover><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">−</mo><mfrac style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000"><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">λ</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">R</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝝁</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">β</mi></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">2</mn></msup></mrow><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">β</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">γ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">+</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">δ</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">Q</mi></msub></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝝁</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">2</mn></msup></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{\alpha}=-\frac{\lambda_{R}\|{\bm{\mu}}_{\beta}\|^{2}}{\beta\gamma(1+\delta_{Q})\|{\bm{\mu}}\|^{2}}</annotation></semantics></math></span>
<span class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></span></span></span>
</span>
</span>
</span></span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F2" class="ltx_figure ltx_align_floatright" style="width:50%;"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.21345v1/optimal-alpha-ridge-N-2000-n-200-gamma_pre-1-gamma_ft-1.svg" id="S4.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:220/160;" width="220" height="160"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 2: </span>Variations of the optimal parameter <math id="S4.F2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha^{\star}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>α</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha^{\star}</annotation></semantics></math> with respect to the alignment between the source <math id="S4.F2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{\mu}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝝁</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{\mu}}</annotation></semantics></math> and target <math id="S4.F2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{\mu}}_{\beta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝝁</mi><mi>β</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{\mu}}_{\beta}</annotation></semantics></math> dataset means. These latter were chosen of norm <math id="S4.F2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.F2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N=2000" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2000</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N=2000</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.F2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=200" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>200</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=200</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.F2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma=\tilde{\gamma}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>=</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>γ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma=\tilde{\gamma}=1</annotation></semantics></math>.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S4.p7" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.p7.1" class="ltx_p">Figure <a href="#S4.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 4 Main results ‣ 𝛼-LoRA: Effective Fine-Tuning via Base Model Rescaling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> shows the evolution of the theoretical test accuracy with the parameter <math id="S4.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> for different source datasets (i.e, different alignments <math id="S4.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math>). In particular, we observe the existence of an optimal parameter <math id="S4.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha^{\star}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>α</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha^{\star}</annotation></semantics></math> that is generally different from <math id="S4.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> (standard approach), and as can be previously anticipated, its impact on the test accuracy is more visible in the case of a higher alignment factor <math id="S4.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math>, which means in this case that we put higher emphasis on the base model to generalize better in the new task (see Remark <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem1" title="Remark 3.1 (About the interpretability of our fine-tuned classifier). ‣ Fine-tuning phase. ‣ 3.1 Theoretical Setting ‣ 3 Problem setting and Background ‣ 𝛼-LoRA: Effective Fine-Tuning via Base Model Rescaling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p8" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.p8.1" class="ltx_p">Focusing on the optimal <math id="S4.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha^{\star}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>α</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha^{\star}</annotation></semantics></math>, Figure <a href="#S4.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 4 Main results ‣ 𝛼-LoRA: Effective Fine-Tuning via Base Model Rescaling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> clearly depicts the non-trivial contribution of the dimension <math id="S4.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> to the choice of <math id="S4.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>. It is clear that <math id="S4.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha^{\star}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>α</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha^{\star}</annotation></semantics></math> is non-decreasing with the alignment <math id="S4.p8.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math> between the source and target task, but its effect gets amplified with the dimension <math id="S4.p8.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> of the problem. Notably, the influence of <math id="S4.p8.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> is more pronounced in low-resource settings (<math id="S4.p8.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p\gg n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>≫</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p\gg n</annotation></semantics></math>) compared to cases where sufficient fine-tuning data is available. This further underscores the crucial role of <math id="S4.p8.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> in effectively leveraging the pre-trained model and source data. Additionally, as <math id="S4.p8.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta\to 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta\to 0</annotation></semantics></math>, we also remark that <math id="S4.p8.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha^{\star}\to 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>α</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha^{\star}\to 0</annotation></semantics></math>, which means that fine-tuning has no added value when the source and target tasks are <span id="S4.p8.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">unrelated</span> and orthogonal.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>Experiments</h2>

<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we present some experiments on real datasets to validate our approach. We start by fine-tuning linear models on the Amazon Review dataset <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Blitzer et al., 2007</a>)</cite> to verify our theoretical findings. After that, we formalize our new class of reparametrization methods and verify its efficiency by experiments on fine-tuning LLMs on the GLUE tasks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2018</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<section id="S5.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.1 </span>Within our theoretical model: Linear Binary Classification</h3>

<div id="S5.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Here we present our experiments on the Amazon Review dataset <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Blitzer et al., 2007</a>)</cite> to validate our theory. This dataset includes several binary classification tasks corresponding to positive versus negative reviews of <span id="S5.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">books</span>, <span id="S5.SS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">dvd</span>, <span id="S5.SS1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">electronics</span>, and <span id="S5.SS1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">kitchen</span>. We apply the standard scaler from <span id="S5.SS1.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">scikit-learn</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Pedregosa et al., 2011</a>)</cite> and estimate <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|{\bm{\mu}}\|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>𝝁</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|{\bm{\mu}}\|</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|{\bm{\mu}}^{\perp}\|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msup><mi>𝝁</mi><mo>⟂</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|{\bm{\mu}}^{\perp}\|</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math> with the normalized data. Figure <a href="#S5.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 5.1 Within our theoretical model: Linear Binary Classification ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ 𝛼-LoRA: Effective Fine-Tuning via Base Model Rescaling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> depicts the variation in test accuracy of three transfer tasks with respect to the parameter <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> and gives a comparison between the three main schemes: <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=0</annotation></semantics></math> (i.e., learning directly on the target data without using previous source knowledge), <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=1</annotation></semantics></math> (classical approach) and with the optimal <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha^{\star}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>α</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha^{\star}</annotation></semantics></math> obtained using the theoretical formula in Theorem <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem4" title="Theorem 4.4 (Optimal 𝛼). ‣ 4 Main results ‣ 𝛼-LoRA: Effective Fine-Tuning via Base Model Rescaling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.4</span></a>. Depending on the tasks, we see a clear improvement in the test accuracy for <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha^{\star}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>α</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha^{\star}</annotation></semantics></math> compared to the other schemes, which further highlights the impact of this scaling parameter. Table <a href="#S5.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 5.1 Within our theoretical model: Linear Binary Classification ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ 𝛼-LoRA: Effective Fine-Tuning via Base Model Rescaling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> summarizes the results obtained for all the possible transfer tasks between the sub-datasets.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.F3" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.21345v1/accuracy_alpha_real-amazon-N-2000-n-40-p-400-gamma_pre-2-gamma_ft-0.1.svg" id="S5.F3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_figure_panel ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:548/125;" width="548" height="125"></object></div><div class="ltx_flex_break"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.21345v1/x1.svg" id="S5.F3.g2" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_figure_panel ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:439/24;" width="439" height="24"></object></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 3: </span>Test accuracy variation with <math id="S5.F3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> for different transfer learning schemes from the Amazon Review dataset <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Blitzer et al., 2007</a>)</cite>. The considered parameters here are: <math id="S5.F3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N=2000" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2000</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N=2000</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.F3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=40" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>40</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=40</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.F3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p=400" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>400</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p=400</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.F3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma=10^{-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma=10^{-1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.F3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\gamma}=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>γ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\gamma}=2</annotation></semantics></math>. </figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="S5.T1" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 1: </span>Test accuracy (in %) comparison over Amazon review datasets <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Blitzer et al., 2007</a>)</cite> for <math id="S5.T1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N=2000" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2000</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N=2000</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.T1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=40" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>40</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=40</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.T1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p=400" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>400</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p=400</annotation></semantics></math>, and optimal regularization parameters <math id="S5.T1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma=\tilde{\gamma}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>=</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>γ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma=\tilde{\gamma}=1</annotation></semantics></math>. As theoretically anticipated, our new fine-tuning approach yields better classification accuracy than training directly on the target dataset (<math id="S5.T1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=0</annotation></semantics></math>) or using <math id="S5.T1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=1</annotation></semantics></math>. The results were computed for <math id="S5.T1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math> random seeds.</figcaption>
<div id="S5.T1.2" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_center ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:397.5pt;height:136.5pt;vertical-align:-66.1pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="transform:translate(-31.9pt,10.9pt) scale(0.861875702332601,0.861875702332601) ;">
<table id="S5.T1.2.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
